---
import { Code as Shiki } from 'astro:components';
import Prose from '../layouts/Prose.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import { PRIMER } from '../lib/lwon/index.ts';
import { accuracy, medianSaving, primerTokens, questionCount } from '../lib/results.ts';
import { url } from '../lib/url.ts';

const typical = medianSaving;
const breakEven = Math.round(primerTokens / typical / 50) * 50;
const main = accuracy.filter((a) => !a.label.includes('effort'));
const total = main.reduce((s, a) => s + a.cells.lwon.total, 0);
const correct = (f: 'json' | 'lwon') => main.reduce((s, a) => s + a.cells[f].correct, 0);
const themes = { light: 'github-light-default', dark: 'github-dark-default' } as const;

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'where', text: 'Where the savings come from' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'before', text: 'Convert before the model reads it' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'primer', text: 'Tell the model about the format' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'writing', text: 'Reading, not writing' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'mcp', text: 'MCP servers' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'accuracy', text: 'Accuracy' },
];
---
<Prose title="For AI" description="How to use LWON to cut the tokens that tool results, API responses and retrieved records cost in LLM prompts." headings={headings}>
  <h1>LWON for AI</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Models read structured data all day: tool results, API responses, database rows, retrieved
    documents. LWON lets them read the same data for fewer tokens, which means lower cost, lower
    latency and more room in the context window.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where">Where the savings come from</h2>
  <p>
    In a JSON array of 100 records, every key is written 100 times, wrapped in quotes and followed by a
    colon. LWON writes each key once in a header and lists only the values after it. The more records
    and the deeper the nesting, the bigger the gap. See the <a href={url('/benchmarks')}>benchmarks</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="before">Convert before the model reads it</h2>
  <p>
    The tokens are saved only if the model never sees the JSON. Encode in your own code, where the data
    arrives: the tool handler, the API client, the retrieval step.
  </p>
  <Shiki lang="ts" themes={themes} code={`import { encode, PRIMER } from 'lwon';

const rows = await db.query('select id, name, plan, mrr from accounts limit 200');

const response = await client.messages.create({
  model: 'claude-sonnet-5-5',
  system: PRIMER,                     // explain the format once
  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: \`Which accounts churned?\\n\\n\${encode(rows)}\` }],
});`} />
  <p>
    Giving the model an <code>encode</code> tool does <strong>not</strong> help: to call it, the model
    would first have to read the JSON, so the tokens are already spent.
  </p>

  <h2 id="primer">Tell the model about the format</h2>
  <p>
    Models do not know LWON yet, so send this primer once per conversation, ideally in the system prompt
    where prompt caching makes it nearly free after the first call. It is about {primerTokens} Claude tokens and
    is exported by the library as <code>PRIMER</code>.
  </p>
  <Code code={PRIMER} lang="text" label="Primer" />
  <p>
    At the typical saving in our benchmarks ({Math.round(typical * 100)}%), the primer pays for itself once
    the data would take about {breakEven} tokens as minified JSON. Below that, send JSON. With a cached
    system prompt the threshold is much lower.
  </p>

  <h2 id="writing">Reading, not writing</h2>
  <p>
    Start by using LWON for data the model <em>reads</em>. Asking a model to <em>write</em> LWON saves
    output tokens, but positional formats are easy to get subtly wrong. If you do, always check the
    output with <code>decode</code> and retry on an error, which includes the line and column.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mcp">MCP servers</h2>
  <p>
    Many MCP servers return large JSON tool results. A proxy that sits between the client and any MCP
    server and converts those results to LWON is planned, so existing servers save tokens without code
    changes. Follow progress on <a href="https://github.com/ahmetzencirli/lwon">GitHub</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="accuracy">Accuracy</h2>
  <p>
    Fewer tokens only help if the model still understands the data. Claude Haiku, Sonnet and Opus 5.5
    each answered {questionCount} questions about the benchmark data in JSON, TOON and LWON. With LWON
    they got <strong>{correct('lwon')} of {total}</strong> right, the same as with JSON ({correct('json')} of {total}).
    With thinking turned down to low effort, Haiku miscounted once in LWON, on how many rows carry an
    optional field. <a href={url('/benchmarks#accuracy')}>Full results</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    These questions sit at the ceiling for current models, so a harder test with repeated trials is
    next. If your prompts need exact counts over sparse data, check them on LWON before switching.
  </p>
</Prose>

<style>
  .lead { margin-bottom: 1.5em; }
</style>
